## Implemented parameters

Units: metres and radians in Python. The GUI presents joint angles in degrees.
`A_i = Rz(theta_i) Tz(d_i) Tx(a_i) Rx(alpha_i)`.

| Joint | d (m) | a (m) | alpha (deg) | theta expressed using GUI q (deg) | Mechanical q range (deg) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | 0.360 | 0 | 90 | -q1 - 90 | -180 to 180 |
| 2 | 0 | 0.355 | 0 | q2 + 90 | -135 to 135 |
| 3 | 0 | 0 | 90 | -q3 + 90 | -155 to 155 |
| 4 | 0.375 | 0 | 90 | q4 + 180 | -200 to 200 |
| 5 | 0 | 0 | -90 | -q5 | -125 to 125 |
| 6 | 0.078 | 0 | 0 | q6 + 90 | -360 to 360 |

Joint signs and offsets are deliberate model conventions (`flip=True` on 1, 3, 5).
Mechanical ranges match the cited specification; smaller software ranges and the
controller's margin restrict the simulated task further.

Mesh assets are `Maryam_Work/RS007N-BASE.STL` and `RS007N-J1.STL` through `J6.STL`.
Record their actual origin, CAD editing work, units and attribution here before
submission. `meshes_are_global_at_home=True` means the assets share the zero-pose
CAD frame; each moving mesh is then transformed by its corresponding joint chain.

`Full_sim.add_maryam_robot` applies the world mounting transform. The current X
mounting coordinate is 14.8 m in the scene, providing clearance beside the serving
tray; it is a workcell placement, not a change to the robot's kinematic dimensions.

